A||||8||FIEROCIOUS||Question for machineists||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||07-08-2000||12:58 AM||||I had new bronze guides recently installed in my Fieros heads to go with my stainless steel valves (I've read from more than one sorce bronze are more compatible with stainless than cast iron)They are the solid valve guides not k-liners (they didn't have the tooling for the k-liners)<P>My question is: When they installed the guide it ended up cracking the area were the valve guide seal goes and on a few actualy broke the old guide off I've been able to pick pieces off with my fingernails! I don't want this in my engine! and the seals get all chewed up when I install them!<P>Is this normal?<BR>Can it be fixed?<BR>Should I have to pay more for it to be fixed or is it their fault?<BR>Do they make positive guide seals with a smaller inside diameter?||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||07-08-2000||09:42 AM||||It sounds like messy work,they should all be the same,no loose chunks of metal.The old guides are cast into the heads,and should've been drilled out to allow the new guides to be pressed in,after that the new guides need to be reamed to the exact size(valve stem + 0.0005).The stem seals should fit right over the new guides,and hopefully you got teflon seals.<BR>Not sure exactly what is happening from your description,but I hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-08-2000||11:59 AM||||I'd say those heads are trash myself. that's major f'd up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2FAST4U||07-08-2000||02:20 PM||||For absolute quality head work by an expert contact:<P>Joel Marsh<BR>Competition Heads<BR>Brea, California  92821<P>(714) 529-8803<P>Joel does heads for some of the biggest names in racing but is still happy to work with the little guys like us. He has years of experience, and the equipment to do the job right the first time. His work is also very reasonably priced. I ship all of my heads and intakes from Denver, Co to California for him to do my work. He also does some very nice intake porting work. Tell him Joe in Denver said to call..<P>Thanks<P>Joe ||||reg||2
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||07-08-2000||05:40 PM||||I installed a "new" valve today and they have more movement than before with old valves! You can definately feel and see movement!!@#*'ed up!<BR>I spent 30 hours porting these heads! The best porting job I've ever done! Is there any way to fix them! maybe find a guide with a larger outside diameter? If I could get a guide the same outside diameter as the positive seal that would fix both problems?<BR>I've done guides at home with k-liners with far better results. When they wanted to use the large guides I asked if they could use the k-liners they said they didn't have the tooling! The only reason I went to them is because no one else would do bronze!<P>Man this sucks. I feel bad for who works the early monday shift!||||reg||11
Z||000005||lowCG||07-08-2000||07:05 PM||||So you're saying that the guides are loose in the head,as well as the valves being loose in the guides?<BR>I guess I'm not reading your posts clearly,but if this is the case,then you need to have some new guides made from bronze,which isn't a terribly big deal.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FIEROCIOUS||07-08-2000||08:41 PM||||Sorry I read my previous posts I don't make sense!<BR>OK I'll try to make it clearer.<BR>The machine shop installed thickwall bronze guides approx 7/16 outside diameter (almost the same diameter as the original machined guide seal) when they drilled the hole to press the new guides in they didn't remove the original seal area. <P>The seal area became paper thin so when they pressed the guides in it cracked and broke away the brittle cast iron in the seal area. The heads aren't ruined I just have cracks were the valve seals go. <P>Now on further inspection the valve to guide clearance is excesive!<BR>Upon even further inspection they didn't bother to clean the guides before they cut the new valve angles and now I have valve guides with metal chips imbedded!<BR>Bronze guides are a little touchy as far as dirt is concerned.(more so than cast iron)<P>Question is do they make valve guides with a thicker outer diameter or should I get new guides and machine the cracked broken cast iron off and find smaller valve seals?<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||07-08-2000||10:37 PM||||I always assumed that the replacement(bronze in your case)guides had their own registers,or grooves for the seals,never heard of doing it the way it's been done to your engine.<BR>It sounds like you need new guides for more than one reason;basically re-do the whole thing over again.<BR>Might be worth talking to the owner of the shop to see if they'd straighten it out,but I'm not sure I'd want go back to a place that did it that way,except to serve a summons to small claims court where you can present pictures,and receipts for the new work during the same visit.Would be cheap insurance to get it fixed perhaps,good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Vader||07-09-2000||10:10 AM||||F,<P>Regarding the myth about stainless valves and their compatibility with cast iron guides, Chevrolet installed stainless valves in CI guide on every LT1 and LT4 engine produced.  I recently did a tear-down on a '94 LT1 with 110K miles and saw no indication of wear whatsoever.  The engine also had a life of hard use on Mobil 1 synthetic.  I installed new Manley 2.02/1.60s in the stock guides with no machining or replacement of the guides.  I expect at least another 100K miles out of the engine now that it is "fresh", albeit at a slightly higher power output.<P>To me, bronze guides are "old school", and since I'm an old fart, I have had more than my share of failures with bronze.  Naval bronze is the only white metal I would use on guides, and most marketers of guides don't have a clue as to the metallurgy of their products. Cast or sintered iron will work fine for me, thank you...<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
